main.content:
{{also|creäte}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|creäte||archaic}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱer- (grow)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|createn}}, from {{der|en|la|creātus}}, the perfect passive participle of {{m|la|creō}}, see {{l|en|-ate|id=verb|pos=verb-forming suffix}}. In this sense, mostly displaced {{ncog|ang|wyrċan}} (whence {{w|Modern English}} {{m|en|work}}) and {{m|ang|ġesċieppan}} (whence {{w|Modern English}} {{m|en|shape}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kɹiːˈeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-create.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪt|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{wikidata lexeme|L9}}
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring into [[existence]]; {{lb|en|sometimes in particular:}}
#: {{syn|en|generate|make}}
#: {{ant|en|annihilate|extinguish}}
#: {{ux|en|You can '''create''' the color orange by mixing yellow and red.}}
#* <!-- this is intended to show use with a "complement" --> '''1829''', Thomas Tully Crybbace, ''An Essay on Moral Freedom'':
#*: [...] God '''created''' man a moral agent.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2012|month=March-April
|author=[[w:Terry Sejnowski|Terrence J. Sejnowski]]
|volume=100|issue=2|page=171|magazine=w:American Scientist
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/well-connected-brains Well-connected Brains]
|passage='''Creating''' a complete map of the human connectome would therefore be a monumental milestone but not the end of the journey to understanding how our brains work.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-21|author=w:Oliver Burkeman
|volume=189|issue=2|page=48|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=[http://www.theguardian.com/technology/2013/may/10/conscious-computing-twitter-facebook-google The tao of tech]
|passage=The dirty secret of the internet is that all this distraction and interruption is immensely profitable. Web companies like to boast about "'''creating''' compelling content", {{...}} "share the things you love with the world" and so on. But the real way to build a successful online business is to be better than your rivals at undermining people's control of their own attention.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Tom Whipple|title=Microcannon firing nanobullets: the future of targeted medicine|magazine=w:The Times|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/science/article/microcannon-firing-nanobullets-the-future-of-targeted-medicine-h8nhjsbd2nd|date=2016-02-01|passage=When an ultrasonic beam is fired at the microcannons, the emulsion evaporates, expanding rapidly into gas. This '''creates''' enough force to push the nanobullets out at velocities reaching several metres per second.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260101063029/https://www.thetimes.com/uk/science/article/microcannon-firing-nanobullets-the-future-of-targeted-medicine-h8nhjsbd2nd|archivedate=1 January 2026}}
## {{lb|en|especially|of a|god}} To bring into [[existence]] out of [[nothing]], without the prior existence of the [[material]]s or [[element]]s used.
##* {{RQ:Geneva Bible|book=Genesis|chapter=I|verse=1|folio=1|passage=In the beginning God '''created''' y{{sup|e}} heauen and the earth. And the earth was without forme & voyde, and darkenes ''was'' vpon the depe, & the Spirit of God moued vpon the waters.}}
## To {{l|en|make#Verb}} or {{l|en|produce#Verb}} from other (e.g. [[raw]], [[unrefined]] or [[scattered]]) [[material]]s or [[combinable]] [[element]]s or [[idea]]s; to [[design]] or [[invest]] with a [[new]] form, shape, function, etc.
##: {{syn|en|invent}}
##: {{ant|en|imitate}}
##: {{ux|en|Couturiers '''create''' exclusive garments for an affluent clientele.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-08|volume=407|issue=8839|page=52|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/international/21579039-chinas-growing-empire-ports-abroad-mainly-about-trade-not-aggression-new-masters The new masters and commanders]
|passage=From the ground, Colombo’s port does not look like much.{{...}} But viewed from high up in one of the growing number of skyscrapers in Sri Lanka’s capital, it is clear that something extraordinary is happening: China is '''creating''' a shipping hub just 200 miles from India’s southern tip.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cause#Verb|cause]], to bring (a non-object) about by an action, behavior, or event, to [[occasion#Verb|occasion]].
#: ''crop failures '''created''' food shortages and high prices''; ''his stubbornness '''created''' many difficulties''
#: {{ux|en|A sudden chemical spill on the highway '''created''' a chain‐collision which '''created''' a record traffic jam.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=7|passage=The turmoil went on—no rest, no peace. {{...}} It was nearly eleven o'clock now, and he strolled out again. In the little fair '''created''' by the costers' barrows the evening only seemed beginning; and the naphtha flares made one's eyes ache, the men's voices grated harshly, and the girls' faces saddened one.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[confer]] or [[invest]] with a [[rank]] or [[title]] of [[nobility]], to [[appoint]], [[ordain]] or [[constitute]].
#: {{ux|en|Henry VIII '''created''' him a Duke. &nbsp; Last month, the queen '''created''' two barons.}}
#: {{ux|en|Under the concordate with Belgium, at least one Belgian clergyman must be '''created''' cardinal; by tradition, every archbishop of Mechelen is thus '''created''' a cardinal.}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=i|page=18|passage='''Create''' him Prorex of Affrica,<br>That he may win the Babilonians hearts,<br>Which will reuolt from Perſean gouernment,<br>Unleſſe they haue a wiſer King than you.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be or do something [[creative]], [[imaginative]], [[originative]].
#: {{ux|en|Children usually enjoy '''creating''', never mind if it is of any use!}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} In [[theatre]], to be the first performer of a role; to [[originate]] a character.
# {{lb|en|UK|intransitive|colloquial}} To make a [[fuss]], [[complain]]; to shout.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1972|author=w:H. E. Bates|title=The Song of the Wren
|passage='What's the time?' she said. 'I must fly. Miss'll start '''creating'''.'}}

