{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A long, thin, flexible length of twisted [[yarns]] ([[strands]]) of [[fibre]] (a [[rope]], for example).
#: {{ux|en|The burglar tied up the victim with a '''cord'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Any quantity of such material when viewed as a mass or commodity.
#: {{syn|en|cordage}}
#: {{ux|en|He looped some '''cord''' around his fingers.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''cord''' of spittle}}
# A small flexible electrical conductor composed of wires insulated separately or in bundles and assembled together usually with an outer cover; the electrical cord of a [[lamp]], [[sweeper]] ({{qualifier|US}} [[vacuum cleaner]]), or other appliance.
# A unit of measurement for [[firewood]], equal to 128 cubic feet (4 &times; 4 &times; 8 feet), composed of logs and/or split logs four feet long and none over eight inches diameter. It is usually seen as a stack four feet high by eight feet long.<!--Move this to separate pages for "full cord" and "bush cord". A full cord of stacked firewood in Canada and USA, or colloquially a "bush cord" in Canada, is 8 by 4 by 4 feet (39 m&sup3;).--><!--A "full cord", or a "bush cord" (as opposed to a "face cord", or a "stove cord"): Is composed of stove-length pieces, i.e. 16 inch; long (4 foot log, perhaps split, subdivided with two cuts), and when stacked has the same face area as a full cord, but only one third the depth; and so the volume is 42.66 cubic feet (16 inches &times; 4 feet &times; 8 feet) (volume = 1.208 m&sup3;)-->
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=The Battering-ram|page=376|passage=Unerringly impelling this dead, impregnable, uninjurable wall, and this most buoyant thing within; there swims behind it all a mass of tremendous life, only to be adequately estimated as piled wood is—by the '''cord'''; and all obedient to one volition, as the smallest insect.}}
#* {{RQ:Asbjornsen Braekstad Tales|chapter=Mackerel Trolling|page=187|passage='If they buy three '''cords''' of birch logs,' said the witch, 'but they must be exact measure—and no bargaining about the price, and if they throw overboard the one '''cord''' of logs, piece by piece, when the first sea comes, and the other '''cord''', piece by piece, when the second sea comes, and the third '''cord''', piece by piece, when the third sea comes, then it's all over with us.'}}
## {{lb|en|collective|by extension}} A quantity of [[firewood]] or cut [[log]]s.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Any [[influence]] by which persons are caught, held, or drawn, as if by a cord.
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=I|poem=To|page=15|passage=Clear-headed friend, whose joyful scorn, / Edged with sharp laughter, cuts atwain / The knots that tangle human creeds, / The wounding '''cords''' that bind and strain / The heart until it bleeds, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Chesnutt House Behind the Cedars|I|passage=Every detail of the house and garden was familiar; a thousand '''cords''' of memory and affection drew him thither; but a stronger counter-motive prevailed.}}
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} Any structure having the appearance of a cord, especially a [[tendon]] or [[nerve]].
#: {{ux|en|'''spermatic''' cord; '''spinal''' cord; '''umbilical''' cord; '''vocal''' cords}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=August 4, 2015|author=Diana Fiorentini; et al.|title=Polyphenols as Modulators of Aquaporin Family in Health and Disease|journal=Oxidative Medicine and Cellular Longevity|url=http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4539495/|doi=10.1155/2015/196914|volume=2015|issue=|pages=
|passage=Since spinal '''cord''' injury is associated with edema and changes in AQP4 expression, Ge and coworkers investigated the potential antiedema effect of epigallocatechin gallate and its underlying mechanism on a rat model of acute spinal '''cord''' injury.}}
