{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱerd-|*dʰeh₁-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|crédit||belief, trust}}, from {{der|en|la|crēditum||a loan, credit}}, neuter of {{m|la|crēditus}}, past participle of {{m|la|crēdere||to believe}}. The verb is from the noun. {{doublet|en|shraddha}}, {{m|en|creed}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɹɛdɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-credit.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛdɪt|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[believe]]; to put [[credence]] in. {{attn|en|British usage?}}
#: {{syn|en|accept|believe}}
#: {{ux|en|Someone said there were over 100,000 people there, but I can't '''credit''' that.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|I|iii|passage=How shall they '''credit''' <br> A poor unlearned virgin?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1777|author=w:Richard Brinsley Sheridan|title=The School for Scandal|section=IV.iii
|passage=[T]he Heart that is conscious of its own integrity is ever slowest to '''credit''' another's Treachery.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1952|author=w:Daphne du Maurier|title=The Apple Tree|chapter=Monte Verità|passage=She said quite naturally, as if nothing had happened, “I want you to go back home, Victor darling. You mustn’t worry about me any more.”’ Victor told me he could hardly '''credit''' it, at first, that she could stand there and say this to him.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|accounting}} To add to an account.
#: {{antonyms|en|debit}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Credit''' accounts receivable with the amount of the invoice.}}
#: {{ux|en|For the payroll period '''credit''' employees' tips to their wages paid account and debit their minimum wage payable account.}}
#: {{ux|en|The full amount of the purchase has been '''credited''' to your account.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To acknowledge the contribution of.
#: {{ux|en|I '''credit''' the town council with restoring the shopping district.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Credit''' the point guard with another assist.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:Architectural Digest|date=May 2026|volume=83|issue=4|title=Nature's Way|author=Cyrus Dunham|page=106
|text=So sublime was the region that many '''credit''' it with being the birthplace of British tourism.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring honour or repute upon; to do credit to; to raise the estimation of.
#* {{RQ:South Twelve Sermons|passage=You '''credit''' the church as much by your government as you did the school formerly by your wit.}}

