===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{senseid|en|seek to achieve or win}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To seek to achieve or win (a prize).
#: {{ux|en|He was '''courting''' big new accounts that previous salesmen had not attempted.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1800|author=[[w:William H. Prescott|William H[ickling] Prescott]]|title=[[gutenberg:34203|History of The Reign of Philip The Second, King of Spain]]|volume=3|passage=On the contrary, they employed the brief respite that was left them in fortifying one another's courage, and in bearing testimony to the truth in so earnest a manner that they might almost seem to have '''courted''' the crown of martyrdom.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1821|author=w:Thomas De Quincey|chapter=To the Reader|title=w:Confessions of an English Opium-Eater|passage=Guilt and misery shrink, by a natural instinct, from public notice: they '''court''' privacy and solitude: and even in their choice of a grave will sometimes sequester themselves from the general population of the churchyard {{...}}}}
# {{senseid|en|run the risk of}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To risk (a consequence, usually negative).
#: {{ux|en|She '''courted''' controversy with her frank speeches.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|month=April|title=Automatic Signalling Problems in an Emergency|journal=Modern Railways|page=273|text=It is not unknown for hot axleboxes to fail completely and for wagons to become derailed as a result. Surely it is '''courting''' disaster to allow a train to proceed for up to seven miles with a defective vehicle before it can be brought to a halt?}}
# {{senseid|en|attempt to attract or invite}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[attempt]] to [[attract]], in any way; to [[invite]] by attractions.
#: {{nearsyn|en|entice|allure|Thesaurus:allure}}
## {{senseid|en|invite for marriage}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To try to win a commitment to marry from.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1590–1592|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=w:The Taming of the Shrew|section=act 1, scene 1|passage=If either of you both love Katharina {{...}} / Leave shall you have to '''court''' her at your pleasure.}}
## {{senseid|en|invite for animal mating}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To engage in behavior conducive to mating with.
##: {{ux|en|The bird was '''courting''' a potential mate by performing an elaborate dance.}}
##: {{ux|en|At this time of year, you can see many animals '''courting'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1849|author=w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|chapter=24|title=The History of England: From the Accession of James II|volume=5|passage=By one person, however, Portland was still assiduously '''courted''', and that person was the king.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1835|author=[[w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Alfred Lord Tennyson]]|title=The Gardener's Daughter|passage={{...}} a well-worn pathway '''courted''' us / To one green wicket in a privet hedge {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=Robert Marshall Grade|title=The Haunted Major
|passage=It is a grim, grey old town, standing on bleak, precipitous cliffs that '''court''' every passing hurricane, {{...}}}}
## {{senseid|en|invite for alliance}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To attempt to gain alliance with.
## {{senseid|en|invite for romance etc}} {{lb|en|intransitive}} To engage in activities intended to win affections.
##: {{syn|en|romance|solicit|Thesaurus:woo}}
##: {{ux|en|She's had a few beaus come '''courting'''.}}

