====Noun====
{{en-noun|~|+|cosmoi}}

# {{senseid|en|universe}}{{lb|en|countable}} The [[universe]] [[regard#Verb|regarded]] as a [[system]] with [[harmony]] and [[order#Noun|order]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Art. V.—The Baron [[w:Alexander von Humboldt|Humboldt]]’s ‘Cosmos.’ The Physical History of the Universe Explained and Displayed.|editor=[[w:J. D. B. De Bow|J[ames] D[unwoody] B[rownson] De Bow]]|journal=[[w:De Bow's Review|De Bow’s Review of the Southern and Western States]]|location=New Orleans, La.|publisher=J. D. B. De Bow|month=August|year=1850|volume=I (3rd Series; volume IX overall)|issue=2|page=153|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=f5wRAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA153|oclc=18037589|passage=This doctrine [the nebular hypothesis] supposes all the material universe to have been once in a fluid or nebular condition, and that, by the operation of universal gravitation and the thousand other laws of nature, the nebular matter has been mainly aggregated into masses, and the existing '''''cosmoi''''' been developed.}}
#* {{RQ:Grote Plato|volume=I|chapter=Speculative Philosophy in Greece, before and in the Time of Sokrates|pages=6–7|pageref=6|passage=It [the earth] was in the centre of the '''Kosmos'''; it remained stationary because of its equal distance from all parts of the outer revolving spheres; there was no cause determining it to move upward rather than downward or sideways, therefore it remained still. Its exhalations nourished the fire in the peripheral regions of the '''Kosmos'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Challenger|page=535|passage=Can you conceive a process by which you, an organic being, are in the same way dissolved into the '''cosmos''', and then by a subtle reversal of the conditions reassembled once more?"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Carl Sagan|chapter=The Shores of the Cosmic Ocean|title=[[w:Cosmos (Sagan book)|Cosmos]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Random House|year=1980|page=4|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/cosmos00saga/page/4/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-394-50294-6|passage=The '''Cosmos''' is all that is or was or ever will be. Our feeblest contemplations of the '''Cosmos''' stir us—there is a tingling in the spine, a catch in the voice, a faint sensation, as if a distant memory, of falling from a height. We know we are approaching the greatest of mysteries.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Andrew Gregory|chapter=The Origins of the Cosmos and of Life: Consider Your Origins|editor=Jon Turney|title=Eureka! The Birth of Science|series=Revolutions in Science|location=Duxford, Cambridgeshire|publisher=Icon Books; Totem Books|year=2001|page=98|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/eurekabirthofsci0000greg/page/98/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-84046-289-0|passage=Along with this question of order was the question of whether there was one '''cosmos''' or many ‘'''''cosmoi'''''’. {{w|Plato}} and {{w|Aristotle}} firmly believed that there was one unique '''cosmos''' that was in some way structured for the best. The atomists, on the other hand, believed that there were many '''''cosmoi''''', separate from one another, in which everything happened by chance.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Terry Horgan|chapter=Materialism, Minimal Emergentism, and the Hard Problem of Consciousness|editors=[[w:Robert C. Koons|Robert C[harles] Koons]]; {{w|George Bealer}}|title=The Waning of Materialism|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=2010|section=part IV (Alternatives to Materialism)|pages=309–310|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/robert-c.-koons-george-bealer-the-waning-of-materialism/page/n327/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-19-955618-2|passage=In seeking a satisfactory formulation of materialism, it helps to employ the notion of a ''possible world''. Possible worlds are plausibly construed not literally as universes other than the single real universe (i.e., not as '''''cosmoi'''''), but rather as ''total ways the '''cosmos''' might be''—i.e., maximal ''properties'' instantiable by the single real world (the single '''cosmos'''). On this usage, the item designated as the actual world—considered as one among the various possible worlds—is not itself the '''cosmos''' either, but rather is the total '''cosmos'''-instantiable property that is ''actually'' instantiated by the '''cosmos'''. But it will be convenient in practice to speak as though the actual world is the '''cosmos''' and as though other possible worlds are other such '''cosmoi''' — a harmless enough manner of speaking, as long as one bears in mind that it is not intended literally.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=Dark energy: A problem of cosmic proportions|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220315220838/https://www.economist.com/science-and-technology/2013/08/24/a-problem-of-cosmic-proportions|date=24 August 2013|volume=408|issue=8850|passage=In Dr [[w:Christof Wetterich|[Christof] Wetterich]]'s picture of the '''cosmos''' the redshift others attribute to expansion is, rather, the result of the universe putting on weight. If atoms weighed less in the past, he reasons, the light they emitted then would, in keeping with the laws of quantum mechanics, have been less energetic than the light they emit now.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[harmonious]], [[ordered#Adjective|ordered]] [[whole#Noun|whole]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=S. B. Palmer|title=Matter and Force in the Oral Cavity|editor=James W[illiam] White|journal=[[w:Journal of the American Dental Association|The Dental Cosmos: A Monthly Record of Dental Science.{{nb...|Devoted to the Interests of the Profession.}}]]|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=The S. S. White Dental Manufacturing Co.,{{nb...|Chestnut Street, corner of Twelfth.}}|date=14 May 1890|year_published=July 1890|volume=XXXII|issue=7|page=538|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dentalcosmos3218whit/page/538/mode/1up|issn=0096-0187|oclc=1566209|passage=This simple cell is a '''cosmos''' in this respect: it represents the laws of the universe in changes of matter, and clearly exemplifies their workings in the oral cavity.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Tobias Baitsch; Amita Bhide|chapter=Politics of Land Use Regulations|editors=Luca Pattaroni; Amita Bhide; Christine Lutringer|title=Politics of Urban Planning: The Making and Unmaking of the Mumbai Development Plan 2014–2034|series=Exploring Urban Change in South Asia|location=Singapore|publisher=[[w:Springer Nature|Springer Nature Singapore]]|year=2022|page=87|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=G99mEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA87|doi=10.1007/978-981-16-8671-9|isbn=978-981-16-8670-2|issn=2367-0045|passage=Obviously, there are multiple '''cosmoses''' in the debate, out of which we picked four. The two first can be understood as compromise '''cosmoses'''. {{...}} There is the '''cosmos''' of the reformer, which took by and large shape in the EDDP and which we entitled ''Public Future''. Then there is the '''cosmos''' of the existing mode of ordering the city which by and large prevailed in the RDDP. {{...}} Further, we present two '''cosmoses''' nurturing the strong opposition. They are both militant perspectives surging from the "civil society".}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Harmony, order.
#: {{antonyms|en|chaos}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle Friedrich|volume=I|chapter=King Friedrich I. again|page=370|passage=He &#91;{{w|Frederick I of Prussia}}&#93; founded Universities, this poor King; University of Halle; Royal Academy of Berlin, [[w:Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz|[Gottfried Wilhelm] Leibniz]] presiding: he fought for Protestantism;—did what he could for the cause of '''Cosmos''' ''versus'' Chaos, after his fashion.}}

