#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Jane Loudon|[Jane] Loudon]]|chapter=Compositæ|title=The Ladies’ Flower-garden of Ornamental Annuals|location=London|publisher=William Smith,{{nb...|113, Fleet Street.}}|year=1840|pages=184–185|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/ladiesflowerg00loud/page/185/mode/1up|oclc=8173364|passage=This beautiful plant was discovered in Mexico, before 1789; as seeds of it sent to Madrid produced plants, which blossomed in that year in the Royal Botanic Garden of Spain. It was first described and figured in 1797, by [[w:Antonio José Cavanilles|[Antonio José] Cavanilles]], who called it '''Cosmos''', from the Greek word Kosmos, beautiful; but this name was afterwards altered by [[w:Carl Ludwig Willdenow|[Carl Ludwig] Willdenow]] to Cosmea, as being more consistent with the rules of botanical nomenclature.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:William Jackson Hooker|title=Tab. 5227. COSMOS DIVERSIFOLIUS; var. ''atro-sanguineus''.|titleurl=https://archive.org/details/mobot31753002721618/page/n19/mode/1up|editor=w:Samuel Curtis|journal=[[w:Curtis's Botanical Magazine|Curtis’s Botanical Magazine, Comprising the Plants of the Royal Gardens of Kew and of Other Botanical Establishments in Great Britain;{{nb...|with Suitable Descriptions}}]]|location=London|publisher=Lovell Reeve & Co.,{{nb...|Henrietta Street, Covent Garden.}}|year=1861|volume=XVII (3rd Series; volume LXXXVII overall)|issn=1355-4905|oclc=973593291|passage=COSMOS DIVERSIFOLIUS; var. ''atro-sanguineus''. ''Various-leaved '''Cosmos'''; deep blood-flowered var.'' {{...}} Seeds of this plant were received by Mr. Thompson, of Ipswich, from Mexico. It is doubtless a handsome species of ''Cosmos''. The question is if it can be safely referred to any described species.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:R. K. Narayan|R[asipuram] K[rishnaswami Iyer] Narayan&#91;aswami&#93;]]|chapter=An Astrologer’s Day|title=An Astrologer’s Day and Other Stories|location=London|publisher=w:Eyre & Spottiswoode|year=1947|page=1|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/astrologersday0000rkna/page/1/mode/1up|oclc=5045757|passage=People were attracted to him as bees are attracted to '''cosmos''' or dahlia stalks.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Frank E. Young (physician)|Frank E[dward] Young]]; w:Margaret Heckler|title=Biological Products; Allergenic Extracts; Implementation of Efficacy Review [21 CFR Parts 600, 610, and 680; Docket No. 81N-0096]|journal=w:Federal Register|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{w|Office of the Federal Register}}, {{w|National Archives and Records Administration}}|date=31 December 1984|year_published=23 January 1985|volume=!50, part II ([[w:United States Department of Health and Human Services|Department of Health and Human Services]], {{w|Food and Drug Administration}})|issue=15|page=3136|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/federal-register-1985-01-23/page/n197/mode/1up|column=3|issn=0097-6326|oclc=690591122|passage=[P]atients with positive skin reactions to short ragweed pollen usually had positive skin reactions to '''cosmos'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Norman Winter|chapter=Annuals: Plants that Hustle|title=Tough-as-nails Flowers for the South|location=Jackson, Miss.|publisher=w:University Press of Mississippi|year=2005|page=24|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=VR3GjqhX1zwC&pg=PA24|column=2|isbn=978-1-57806-543-1|passage=The '''cosmos''' gives a perennial-like performance by reseeding, although the resulting flower may be different than the parent.}}

